{"abstract":"Aim: Heart Failure with preserved Ejection Fraction (HFpEF) is characterized by diastolic dysfunction and reduced cardiac output, but its pathophysiology remains poorly understood. Animal models of HFpEF are challenging due to difficulties in assessing the degree of heart failure in small animals. This study aimed at inducing HFpEF in a mouse model to probe preload-dependency. Methods: Increased body mass and arterial hypertension were induced in mice using a Western diet and NO synthase inhibition. Preload dependence was tested ex vivo. Results: Mice with obesity and hypertension exhibited reduced cardiac output, indicating a failing heart. Increased left ventricular filling pressure during diastole suggested reduced compliance. Notably, the ejection fraction was preserved, suggesting the development of HFpEF. Spontaneous physical activity at night was reduced in HFpEF mice, indicating exercise intolerance; however, the cardiac connective tissue content was comparable between HFpEF and control mice. The HFpEF mice showed increased vulnerability to reduced preload ex vivo, indicating that elevated left ventricular filling pressure compensated for the rigid left ventricle, preventing a critical decrease in cardiac output. Conclusion: This animal model successfully developed mild HFpEF with a reduced pump function that was dependent on a high preload. A model of mild HFpEF may serve as a valuable tool for studying disease progression and interventions aimed at delaying or reversing symptom advancement, considering the slow development of HFpEF in patients.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Jacobsen, Jens Christian Brings","Schubert, Irene H.","Larsen, Karin","Terzic, Dijana","Thisted, Louise","Thomsen, Morten B."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-732831"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Compliance","Diastolic dysfunction","Fibrosis","Heart failure","High fat diet","L NAME","Obesity"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"preload dependence in an animal model of mild heart failure with preserved ejection fraction hfpef","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/apha.14099","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-732831","identifier":[{"id":"85182448950","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38230889","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-f2176291-aba6-48ce-a980-5c155d0a4eac"],"keyword":["L-NAME","compliance","diastolic dysfunction","fibrosis","heart failure","high-fat diet","obesity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A49F3F336E35747E8F6EA960590A01D6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/apha.14099","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/386368982/Preload_dependence.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Renal and Vascular Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshosp